  "body": {
    "id": "thm:local-minimax-lower-bound",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "If \\(\\mathcal A\\in\\mathfrak A_{\\mathrm{det}}\\) has deterministic limiting rate \\(r\\in\\mathcal R_B\\), then \\(\\mathcal R_-(\\mathcal A)\\ge L(r)\\ge V^*\\). The supremum in \\(\\mathcal R_-\\) ranges over the entire local fiber before \\(C_v\\to\\infty\\), and the claim does not require regularity or convergence of the terminal policy selector.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(\\mathfrak A_{\\mathrm{det}}\\)",
      "\\(r\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal R_B\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal R_-(\\mathcal A)\\)",
      "\\(L(r)\\)",
      "\\(V^*\\)",
      "\\(v\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:local-chart-membership",
      "thm:nuisance-uniform-experiment",
      "thm:gaussian-value-characterization"
    ],
    "justification": "The theorem supplies the minimax converse against every measurable deterministic-limit annotation procedure.",
    "gap": "Hirano and Porter do not optimize an observation design, and Nwankwo et al. optimize scalar estimation variance rather than terminal policy regret.",
    "consumer": "The lower bound certifies that no alternative fixed-limit targeted follow-up scheme can uniformly beat the proposed value.",
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(\\mathcal A\\in\\mathfrak A_{\\mathrm{det}}\\) have deterministic limiting rate \\(r\\in\\mathcal R_B\\), and fix a finite prior \\(\\nu=\\sum_{s=1}^Nw_s\\delta_{h_s}\\) on \\(\\mathcal H\\).  The finite-prior part of \\(\\mathrm{thm:nuisance\\text{-}uniform\\text{-}experiment}\\) supplies its least-favourable submodels \\(q^0+\\mathcal T_rh_s/\\sqrt n\\), places both signs in \\(\\mathcal M_\\eta(p)\\) eventually, and places the finitely many corresponding nuisance coordinates in a common finite ball.  By \\(\\mathrm{thm:local\\text{-}chart\\text{-}membership}\\), that ball is contained in the full local fiber for all sufficiently large \\(n\\).  Denote its radius by \\(C(\\nu)<\\infty\\).\n\nFor every \\(C_v\\geq C(\\nu)\\), the supremum defining \\(\\mathcal R_-(\\mathcal A)\\) contains this finite submodel.  A supremum dominates a prior average, and the Bayes envelope dominates no particular terminal rule.  The finite-prior transfer in \\(\\mathrm{thm:nuisance\\text{-}uniform\\text{-}experiment}\\) therefore gives\n\\[\n \\mathcal R_-(\\mathcal A)\\geq B_r(\\nu),                              \\tag{1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(B_r(\\nu)\\) is the Bayes regret in \\(Z\\sim N(h,\\Sigma(r))\\).  This step uses no convergence or regularity of the terminal selector: pointwise minimization supplies the Bayes envelope for every measurable selector.\n\nTaking the supremum of (1) over finite priors and invoking the finite-prior equality in \\(\\mathrm{thm:gaussian\\text{-}value\\text{-}characterization}\\) yields\n\\[\n \\mathcal R_-(\\mathcal A)\\geq\\sup_{\\nu\\ {\\rm finite}}B_r(\\nu)=L(r). \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe order of limits is the advertised one: \\(n\\to\\infty\\) is taken for a fixed ball containing the chosen finite prior, and only afterward does \\(C_v\\to\\infty\\).  Finally \\(r\\in\\mathcal R_B\\), so the definition \\(V^*=\\min_{s\\in\\mathcal R_B}L(s)\\) gives\n\\[\n \\mathcal R_-(\\mathcal A)\\geq L(r)\\geq V^*.\n\\]",
